  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAs a sign of the changing seasons, the warm front has definitely\ngotten bogged down trying to lift thru the forecast area today.\nThat is providing a focus for some sprinkles that continue to\nwork east southeast across the area. They are fighting some dry\nair still, so not much accumulation is taking place just yet.\n\nSlowly but surely we saturate and more showers will begin\nreaching the ground. I think nearly everywhere will accumulate\nsome rainfall thru tonight, but I do not see significant drought\nrelief in store for anywhere. There highest totals will be in\nthe higher terrain where some upslope enhancement is possible on\nthe southwest facing slopes. Even then I have a hard time seeing\nmuch more than 0.5 to 0.75 inches. Rain will end from northwest\nto southeast by sunrise. \n\nNorthwesterly winds will then increase with daytime heating.\nForecast soundings continue to indicate that mixing up to 30\nkt is possible, so I have maintained the trend of bumping up\nNBM wind gusts for widespread 30 to 35 mph gusts. The upper trof\nand coldest air aloft are not forecast to cross the area until\nThu evening. So daytime temps will be quite warm, especially\nnear the coast where some downslope warming helps get readings\ninto the 80s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nLow pressure makes it's exit off the Canadian coast and into \nthe northern North Atlantic Friday. Meanwhile, high pressure \nbegins to make it's way into the Northeast which briefly \ntightens the pressure gradient and makes for a breezy day with \ngusts 20-25 mph. Northwesterly flow brings in a colder airmass \naloft with 850 mb temperatures only around +5 to +8 C. Full sun \nshould allow us to mix down the full potential with high \ntemperatures in the 60s north of the mountains, and low to \nmid-70s south of the mountains. Temperatures are going to \nquickly drop Friday night as winds go calm and clouds remain \nsparse. I think we easily decouple with at least widespread low \nto mid-40s a very likely solution by early Friday morning. I \nhave blended in some cooler MOS guidance to reflect this, but I \nwouldn't be surprised if we have to go even colder as more hi- \nres guidance that better handles raditational cooling becomes \navailable.\n\nHigh pressure slides overhead Saturday coinciding with upper \nlevel ridging, allowing for high temperatures to end up in the \n70s across the area as 850 mb temperatures rise to +10 to +12 C \nwith continued clear skies aiding in maxing out mixing potential\nonce again. High pressure slides east through the day and the \nresulting southwesterly flow advects a warmer and more moist \nairmass into the region. Low temperatures Saturday night stand \nto end up in the upper 40s and 50s as clouds increase through \nthe night.\n\nGlobal models have come into good agreement on a cold front \napproaching the area Sunday which brings about our next chance \nfor showers, albeit low. The signal in the ensembles is \nincreasing, but it is still is not great so continued low chance\nPoPs seem appropriate. High pressure returns to start next week\noff dry, but another signal is emerging toward midweek and this\none initially appears stronger. Models have some subtle timing \ndifferences, but all suggest another seasonably strong low \ndragging a front through the Northeast. Trends will continue to \nbe monitored.",
